# Essay 12 — Crime, Poverty and Education

## Question

The increase in crime rates is mainly caused by poverty and lack of education. To what extent do you agree or disagree?

## Sample Answer

Some people argue that rising crime rates are mainly caused by poverty and a lack of education. I largely agree with this opinion because financial difficulties and limited educational opportunities can increase the risk of criminal behaviour. However, I believe that other factors also contribute to crime.

Poverty can be an important cause of crime because people with very low incomes may struggle to meet their basic needs. In some cases, they may turn to theft or other illegal activities to obtain money. Lack of education can make this situation worse because people with limited qualifications may have fewer opportunities to find stable employment. For example, a young person who leaves school early may have difficulty finding a well-paid job and could become involved in criminal activities.

Nevertheless, poverty and education are not the only causes of crime. Family problems, drug addiction and negative peer influence can also encourage people to break the law. For instance, teenagers who spend time with criminal groups may copy their behaviour even if they come from financially stable families. In addition, some crimes are committed because of personal anger or a desire for revenge.

In conclusion, I agree that poverty and a lack of education are major causes of increasing crime rates. However, governments should also address family problems, drug abuse and harmful social influences in order to reduce crime effectively.

